Rules:
- Emit at most <%= max_theses %> theses.
- The allowance is a hard ceiling, not a target. The deterministic proposal
  leverage floor is <%= format("%.4f", min_leverage_score) %>. Before emitting,
  estimate the candidate score from the measured breakdown multiplied by each
  bounded relief fraction; return zero theses when it cannot meet the floor.
- Returning zero theses is the correct result when the slice shows no material,
  recurring architecture cost. Do not manufacture a proposal to fill the
  allowance.
- A current architecture cost needs current consequence evidence: confirmed
  behavioral divergence, three or more independently owned copies of the same
  decision, repeated co-change visible in history, caller branching caused by
  a boundary leak, or a measured defect/coverage consequence. “Can drift,”
  naming inconsistency, or duplicated mechanics without an observed consequence
  is not sufficient.
- Prefer changes that eliminate duplicated decisions, unstable dependencies,
  boundary leaks, or repeated cross-component edits. A file split, rename,
  wrapper, helper extraction, or class extraction is not valuable by itself;
  omit it unless the evidence shows which meaningful ownership or dependency
  cost it removes.
- For a consolidation or extraction, identify the ownership decision or
  dependency direction the proposal deletes. Moving mechanics into a new
  helper, collaborator, registry, or taxonomy without deleting such a decision
  is added indirection, not leverage; omit it.
- Refactoring preserves the public contract by definition: APIs, CLI behavior,
  schemas, protocols, routes, data formats, infrastructure outputs, and exit
  behavior must remain identical. Working *inside* files that host that
  contract is fine. If a proposal cannot avoid changing the contract, it is not a refactor thesis —
  either drop it or declare `risk.public_api_impact: true` with details so it
  is reported as out-of-contract rather than accepted.
- Every evidence item must set `file` to one repository-relative path string —
  the key is singular `file`, never a `files` array. A thesis with no
  file-backed evidence item is retained but flagged inadmissible.
- Every admissible thesis needs one coherent evidence item with `file`, a
  concrete `claim`, and either a positive `line` or non-empty `snippet`.
- Never copy feature-wide totals into file or line evidence. Evidence items do
  not accept `signal` or `value`; the measured totals above describe the entire
  feature, not the cited line.
- Include `expected_leverage.drivers`. Each driver names one measured signal
  from churn, fan_in, complexity, coupling, bug_density, or coverage_gap; gives
  a `relief` fraction from 0 through 1; and explains the proposal-specific
  `mechanism`. Use each signal at most once. Hive ignores agent-authored scores
  and deterministically derives score and breakdown from hotspot contribution *
  bounded relief.
- Copy the feature boundary from the mapped feature unless you have a concrete
  reason to flag a cross-feature risk.
- Include required_validation.commands using the available command names. An
  automatic fix requires at least one executable project command. If this
  slice lacks useful tests, set characterization_first true, explain the test
  needed before refactoring, and include a broader configured behavior command
  when one is genuinely applicable. With no applicable command the thesis is
  report-only, so emit it only when the architecture cost is still material.
